# Nova.Help — local configuration.
#
# Copy this file to `.env` and fill in what you need. `npm start` and `npm run dev` load it
# automatically when it exists (Node's own --env-file-if-exists; there is no dotenv package).
#
#   cp .env.example .env
#
# `.env` is gitignored. Never commit it: it holds a client secret.
# Every variable here is optional — with none of them set, Nova.Help runs on email, password
# and guest tickets exactly as it always has.

# ── The basics ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# PORT=4400
# HOST=127.0.0.1

# Where tickets and accounts are stored. Default ./var
# NOVA_HELP_DATA=./var

# Set this in any real deployment. It signs ticket passes and account sessions, and if it is
# unset one is generated into var/secret — which is fine for one process and wrong for two.
# Generate one with:  node -e "console.log(require('crypto').randomBytes(32).toString('hex'))"
# NOVA_HELP_SECRET=

# Set when running behind a reverse proxy, so rate limiting sees real client addresses.
# NOVA_HELP_TRUST_PROXY=1

# ── Google sign-in ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# BOTH of the next two must be set for the "Continue with Google" button to appear. Set only
# one and Nova.Help logs a warning and ignores it, because a button that leads to an error page
# is worse than no button.
#
# To get them: https://console.cloud.google.com/apis/credentials
#   1. Create (or pick) a project.
#   2. Configure the OAuth consent screen. External is fine. Scopes: email, profile, openid.
#      While it is in "Testing" only the accounts you list as test users can sign in.
#   3. Credentials -> Create credentials -> OAuth client ID -> Web application.
#   4. Under "Authorised redirect URIs" add EXACTLY the callback below, including the port.
#      Google matches this string literally — a trailing slash or the wrong host will fail.
#
#        http://127.0.0.1:4400/account/auth/google/callback
#
#      (Google accepts http:// and 127.0.0.1 for local development. For a real deployment use
#       your https origin, e.g. https://nova.help/account/auth/google/callback)
#   5. Copy the client ID and client secret into the two lines below.
#
# NOVA_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=000000000000-xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx.apps.googleusercontent.com
# NOVA_GOOGLE_SECRET=GOCSPX-xxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxxx

# The public origin, used to build the redirect URI above. Without it each request infers one
# from its own Host header, which is fine locally. Set it in production.
# NOVA_HELP_ORIGIN=http://127.0.0.1:4400
